#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "3ee74da8-3a3b-5119-b34f-aee3f5770302" :question/math-academy-id "e-21832" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\sinh(3t)$." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\sinh(3t) = \\dfrac{e^{3t} - e^{-3t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{\\sinh (3t)} &= L{\\frac{e^{3t} - e^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\\n&= L{\\frac{e^{3t}}{2} - \\frac{e^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\\n&= L{\\frac{e^{3t}}{2}} - L{\\frac{e^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}L{e^{3t}} - \\frac{1}{2}L{e^{-3t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{e^{kt}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-k}, \\qquad s > k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{3t}} &= \\frac{1}{s - 3}, s > 3 \\\\\nL{e^{-3t}} &= \\frac{1}{s + 3}, s > - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\sinh(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{\\sinh (3t)} &= \\frac{1}{2}L{e^{3t}} - \\frac{1}{2}L{e^{-3t}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s - 3} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s - 3} - \\frac{1}{s + 3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(((s + 3) - (s - 3))/((s - 3)(s + 3))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{6}{s^{2} - 9}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{s^{2} - 9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathcal L\\{e^{3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty)$ and $\\mathcal L \\{e^{-3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty)$, we have that $\\mathcal L\\{\\sinh(3t)\\}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty) \\cap (-3,\\infty) = (3,\\infty)\n$$\n\nthat is, $s > 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147296 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/id #uuid "c012d177-e70b-5332-b5b2-91bf9b829eca" :question/math-academy-id "e-6647" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-3t}-e^{5t}$ for $s>5$." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-3t} - e^{5t}} &= L{e^{-3t}} + L{-e^{5t}} \\\\\n&= L{e^{-3t}} - L{e^{5t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{e^{kt}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-k}, \\qquad s > k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-3t}} &= \\frac{1}{s + 3}, s > - 3 \\\\\nL{e^{5t}} &= \\frac{1}{s - 5}, s > 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= L{e^{-3t}} - L{e^{5t}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s + 3} - \\frac{1}{s - 5} \\\\\n&= ((s - 5) - (s + 3))/((s + 3)(s - 5)) \\\\\n&= (-8)/((s + 3)(s - 5)) \\\\\n&= - (8)/((s + 3)(s - 5))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathcal L\\{e^{-3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty)$ and $\\mathcal L \\{e^{5t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty)$, we have that $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-3,\\infty) \\cap (5,\\infty) = (5,\\infty)\n$$\n\nthat is, $s > 5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147321 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "db1ffc3b-835c-5b53-bd41-802a7841e3ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-6736" :question/problem "Given that for $s > 0$,\n$L{\\sin ωt} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, L{\\cos ωt} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}$,\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 3\\cos{9t} - 2\\sin{9t}$ for $s > 0$." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{3\\cos 9t - 2\\sin 9t} &= L{3\\cos 9t} + L{-2\\sin 9t} \\\\\n&= 3L{\\cos 9t} - 2L{\\sin 9t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt} &= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{\\cos 9t} &= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81}, s > 0 \\\\\nL{\\sin 9t} &= \\frac{9}{s^{2} + 81}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= 3L{\\cos 9t} - 2L{\\sin 9t} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{9}{s^{2} + 81} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3s}{s^{2} + 81} - \\frac{18}{s^{2} + 81} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3s - 18}{s^{2} + 81}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b323ec43-0827-5b71-8859-662f90d2632b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :a